From 57f5fac23666f02d2c1c631ffb321c377c6e51c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:21:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge: send Dolby Vision as certified source devices
 send it

ETSI GS CCM 001 V1.1.1 defines what goes through the tunnel: the HDR
picture with its DM metadata, the metadata describing that picture
(6.2.2: the YCCtoRGB matrix and offsets "for the conversion from the
opponent colour representation (YCbCr, ICtCp) to the non-linear
trichromatic colour representation (RGB, LMS) of the HDR output
picture", RGBtoLMS, and "If the HDR picture is the output picture of the
composing process as defined in clause 5.4", signal_bit_depth =
hdr_bit_depth and signal_color_space = "the corresponding colour
representation method of the associated HDR output picture"). The bridge
did not send that picture: it scaled the composer's 4:2:0 chroma to 4:4:4,
decoded it to RGB, linearized it, went through LMS to BT.2020 RGB, clipped
it to [0, 1] and converted it to BT.2020 YCbCr 4:2:2, with a header for
BT.2020 that it wrote itself. On the way it clamps every component of T'
outside [0, 1] and, through libplacebo's float LMS -> RGB matrix times the
RPU's 14-bit RGBtoLMS (the identity only to about 1e-4), moved light from
the bright components of a pixel into the dark ones (libplacebo-9939 of
the LibreELEC tree now leaves that round trip out where the RPU's RGB is
BT.2020); for profile 5 (IPT-PQ-c2) it is also a change of colour space.
A certified source makes none of these.

Now, by default, the picture of the composer (libplacebo's
pl_render_params.dovi_ccm, which composes as clause 5.4 does) goes out as
it is, in the colour representation of its RPU (IPT in the places of Y,
Cb and Cr for profile 5, 5.2.1 note), 12-bit 4:2:2, and the DM header
carries the RPU's own YCCtoRGB matrix and offsets, RGBtoLMS,
signal_bit_depth = hdr_bit_depth and signal_color_space (table 6); the
reserved bytes stay as the clause has them (0xFFFF, eight zeros, 0x01
0x01, 0x00 0x2A). The extension blocks are those of before.

- libplacebo composes the planes into textures of the bridge and draws
  nothing else (pl_render_params.dovi_ccm_planes and dovi_ccm_only,
  libplacebo-9932 of the LibreELEC tree), in both ways: the 4:4:4
  scaling, the colour conversion and its transcendentals are not made.
- The 4:2:2 rows of chroma come from the 4:2:0 ones by a vertical filter
  at the phases of the chroma location (libplacebo's plane shift: on the
  sample and at 1/2 for top left, the UHD Blu-ray one; at +-1/4 for left
  and centre; chroma that is not on the even luma column is moved onto it
  first). The clause gives no filter. Lanczos with a = 6 (integer weights
  over 2^14), chosen by measurement against the band-limited interpolation
  in float64 of the composer's chroma of 8 pictures of a profile 7 FEL
  stream and of band-limited noise, among the Lanczos windows 2 to 8,
  bilinear (annex B.3's chroma filter), annex B.2's 6 and 8 taps and
  Catmull-Rom (annex B.3's luma filter): the shortest Lanczos whose
  magnitude response stays within 3 % of 1 up to 0.4 cycles per sample at
  every phase (0.973 to 1.022; Lanczos 5: 0.904 to 1.024, bilinear down to
  0.309), and the longest that stays within the 6 chroma rows around the
  active picture that the composer writes when it leaves out the rows of
  the bars (pl_render_params.active_rows). RMS error on the real pictures,
  codes of 4095, left and top-left locations: 0.235 and 0.205 (Lanczos 8:
  0.227 and 0.195; bilinear: 0.378 and 0.377); on noise up to half the
  chroma Nyquist rate: 0.53 and 0.40 (bilinear: 25.3 and 23.9).
- The packer (dvbridge_source.c) works in integers from the composed
  planes on: the rows of 4:2:2 in a texture of 16-bit integers (even rows,
  then odd ones, drawn apart so that each draw runs one filter), then per
  pixel its luma, the chroma of its pair, black of the transported
  representation (the RPU's offsets as codes: 256/2048/2048 for limited
  YCbCr, 0/2048/2048 for IPT) outside the active picture, and the
  metadata bit with its parity.
- What Kodi draws over the video (the GUI, PQ subtitles) it draws over a
  picture that the packer makes from the transport itself: its codes
  decoded with the chain the header describes, to PQ BT.2020 RGB (for
  profiles 7 and 8, whose RGBtoLMS is BT.2020's, T' itself; for IPT
  through the LMS of the header with RGBtoLMS_2020^-1 rgb_to_lms, the
  exact 14-bit matrices; the chroma of an odd pixel halfway between its
  pair's and the next pair's), within [0, 1] as any picture of Kodi's,
  alpha 2. Kodi's blend functions leave a pixel drawn over with a
  colour + (1 - a) picture and an alpha of
  2 - a (the GUI composite's alpha factors are a + dst (1 - a)), so the
  packer knows which pixels were drawn over and how much. It recomputes
  the picture and converts them back with the exact inverse of that
  chain, as Kodi blended them: to_source(blended) + (1 - a) (codes -
  to_source(picture)), whose second term makes it the codes of the
  picture as a goes to 0 whatever the GPU's float arithmetic loses there
  and back (for profiles 7 and 8 also where the picture is clamped: the
  second term carries the codes). Where decoding is not invertible (IPT
  with a component of T' or of the picture out of [0, 1], which it
  clamps), the colour that was drawn is taken from the blended RGB,
  converted and blended with the codes of the picture:
  (1 - a) codes + a to_source(colour). The luma of each pixel comes from
  its own, the chroma of a pair from its even pixel; a pixel nothing was
  drawn over keeps the composer's samples bit for bit. Captures read that
  picture; its alpha is clamped to 1 by the readback.
- A picture that this transport does not cover goes out converted, as
  before, with its header: one that is scaled (the composer's picture is
  not the one on the raster), one that libplacebo does not compose as the
  clause does, a composer output of other than 12 bits, matrices or
  offsets not in the units of the header. Why is logged once per reason.
- With the source transport the direct way also takes a picture drawn at
  its own size elsewhere on the raster (a 3840x1600 encode), and frames
  without GUI elements take it unless the drawing path is set to always
  composed.

With the rest of native output:
- libplacebo's step-down of the composer of the clause applies to the
  composition of these pictures (levels 1 and 2 resample the enhancement
  layer more cheaply; level 3 scales up nothing here, so
  dvbridge_render_step_levels() gives 0x7 with the residual and 0x1
  without when the last picture went out so); their GPU time is the one
  the step-down goes by, but not one of the two ways that the choice of
  the way compares (the GPU timer of a frame says which: path).
- The enhancement layer's chroma is placed on the base layer's grid
  (align_layer_chroma()) for this composition too: the same image is
  composed whichever transport it goes out in.
- The check of the bytes (dvverify): the reference renderer takes the
  transport from the renderer of the frame it checks
  (dvbridge_render_reference(); it changes the bytes on purpose),
  prepares the source transport's candidate from that renderer's
  transport state as it does the converted one's, and packs its composed
  picture in its transport (dvbridge_render_pack()). The switches of the
  source transport that are to give the same bytes are off there: the
  rows of the bars left out of the composition (active_rows, with the
  bars written first), and the 4:2:2 chroma made only of the rows and
  columns that the transport reads (dvbridge_source_rows(), all: the
  reference makes every one, so that a wrong limit is found). CDVVerify
  tells the kinds of
  render apart by their transport too (the first picture in each uses
  other programs), and a check draws its reference the way the frame is
  drawn: TriesDirect() decides as Prepare() does, so a picture at its own
  size placed on the raster is checked directly.
- The warm-up before the first frame (dvbridge_render_warm()) makes the
  source transport's programs ready: the composed and the direct way (the
  direct one needs no quad operations here, and takes a picture at its own
  size anywhere on the raster, as the frames do), with the residual and
  without it, at the levels that change anything in the frame's transport
  (known after its first render: 0 to 2 with the residual and 0 without;
  converted, 0 to 3 and 0 and 3), and packs one composed picture in its
  transport. The packer's own programs are linked when the renderer is
  made. It gives back the transport of the last picture with the rest of
  the renderer's state.
- The packets are 32 words each, four bytes to a word, up to 16;
  dvbridge_prepare() stays as it is and dvbridge_prepare_transport()
  takes the transport.

Setting "Transport" (videoscreen.hadesdvtransport), in the group "Dolby
Vision engine" (System > Display, expert), after "Composition": as
certified players (the default) or converted to BT.2020. Read when native
output starts with the others of the group (DVEngineRead(),
CDVEngineSettings::sourceTransport; CDVBridgeGLES::UseSourceTransport(),
which a renderer made later takes too), never in the middle of a video,
and told in the log of the group. With the pictures converted, what the
fidelity step-down measures is not kept (StepKept(): the levels it keeps
for a picture format are of the work of the default transport); what the
choice of the way measures is of converted frames either way, and is
kept. The Dolby Vision page of the player information says which
transport the frames go out in. Strings: core 39894 to 39897 (shown from
Estuary 32000 to 32003), Estuary 32000 to 32004, in English and the three
Spanish ones.

Measured on Mesa's software OpenGL ES (zink on lavapipe), not on a GPU or
a display, on the integrated tree with this commit and the next one (the level 5
block taken out to what is drawn over the bars; where nothing is, it
changes nothing), with the libplacebo of the LibreELEC tree (patches
9913, 9914, 9920, 9930, 9931, 9939, 9940) and 9932, on 8 pictures of a
profile 7 FEL stream (top-left chroma), each with its RPU as authored and
with the colour metadata of profile 5 (IPT) and of full-range YCbCr, at
three chroma locations, five active areas, a 3840x1600 picture at row
280, a scaled one and a 10-bit composer output (176 drawings):
- the two ways give the same bytes in all of them;
- in the source transport, every byte is that of a reference written
  apart in the test (its own Lanczos weights, black, bit embedding), from
  the planes of a C transcription of the composer (144 drawings with
  those planes, sample for sample the same as the GPU's);
- the DM header is the one of table 3 for the RPU, field by field (160 of
  160), and the RPU parsed as dovi_tool parses it gives the same fields
  (8 of 8);
- the pictures it does not cover have the converted transport's bytes
  (16 of 16);
- with things drawn over the picture with Kodi's blend functions (text,
  premultiplied overlays with an odd edge, a GUI panel over the bars, an
  opaque block, alpha 1/255 and 2^-20; 40 drawings): every value of a
  pixel nothing was drawn over keeps its bytes (640,545,160 values); the
  picture under the drawing is the transport decoded in float64 and
  clamped to [0, 1], to 0.164 code of 4095 (float32 powers, for IPT);
  every value converted back (23,006,840) is that of the float64 formula
  within one code (416,676 off by one, none by more); at an opacity of
  2^-20 every value is the picture's (640,000); decoding and its inverse
  agree to 2.3e-10 code before rounding; and a profile 5 RPU made to have
  L' above 1 and M' below 0 in every pixel (decoding clamps them) passes
  the same;
- 24 mutations of the transport each make the test fail, or do what is
  expected of them: the phases of the vertical filter, Lanczos 3 for 6,
  black, the colour space of the header, the coverage of a pixel and of
  its pair, the pair's pixel, the form where decoding is not invertible
  and its guard, the clamp of the picture, the opacity, the chroma of an
  odd pixel, the domain of decoding, parity, the LMS inverse, the affine
  shortcut, a row of the luma, chroma rows past the picture, rows of the
  active picture left out, bars, alpha, re-siting (22 caught); an inverse
  made 4 parts in 10^4 too large keeps the codes at 2^-20 with the
  (1 - a) term and does not without it (2 as expected); and 6 of the
  hooks, with the test of the check of the bytes for this transport: the reference
  drawn in its own transport, packed by the converted packer, prepared
  from its own transport state, the warm-up drawing levels that change
  nothing here, and rows of the active picture left out of the 4:2:2
  chroma (each found), and the last with the reference limiting its rows
  as the frames do (not found, as it should not be: the reference's
  every row is what finds it);
- with the setting on BT.2020, the render test of the bridge
  (dvrender_test_merged.c, hashing the colour of its float picture) gives
  the hashes of the bridge before it in every drawing (230 lines);
- the check of the bytes: its test gives, converted, the
  same transports and results here as before it (37 lines); its copy
  for the source transport finds all 20 checks of two pictures identical
  (directly and composed, kept on the screen, without bars; placed and
  elsewhere, scaled, converted); the warm-up made 8 renders and compiled
  9 programs, after which none of five frames drawn as a session draws
  them compiled one (without it: 4, 0, 1, 2 and 2), with the same
  transports frame by frame as without it, and with the cache of a
  session before it compiled none (9 taken from it); converted, 12 renders
  and 14 programs, as before it;
- the engine settings: their host test with the transport
  (every value, what is kept, "all automatic", the log), 523 checks.
This commit alone: the same test, its quick run (one picture, 13
drawings, without the level 5 sequences), passes; its sources compile.
What the converted transport loses that this one does not: its clamp of
T' (in the 8 pictures, 6.3 million pixels with a component below 0 and
1664 above 1; up to all 6.2 million above 1 in picture 1509 of the
stream), the 4:4:4 round trip of the chroma, and, for profile 5, the
change of colour space. (libplacebo's LMS round trip, which moved up to
7.8 codes of 4095 in the components of the 8 pictures, mean 0.12, and up
to 469 to 679 codes on pictures 47, 704, 930, 1508 and 1509, measured on
the composer's own codes in float64, is no longer made for an RPU whose
RGB is BT.2020: libplacebo-9939 of the LibreELEC tree.) The direct way of
a 3840x2160 picture is now the composer's 4 passes and the packer's 2 (3
draws; one more for horizontally centred chroma), where it was those 4
and 2 of libplacebo: about 44 million texel fetches a frame for top-left
chroma (66 million for left), where the 4:4:4 scaling and the conversion
took about 83 million and 12 transcendental functions a pixel; with
something drawn over it, one pass more, the picture, in place of
libplacebo's whole reconstruction. (Wall clock on lavapipe, a CPU shared
with other work, per drawing: converted 0.065 s direct, 0.092 s
composed; this transport 0.077 and 0.098 s. Not a measure of a GPU.)
What only the display can tell: whether it takes the composer's picture
as the header describes it (colours, profile 5 in IPT, T' out of [0, 1]
as sent), and the transitions when the GUI appears and goes.
